            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing scoops instead of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Body Fortress scoop has 30 g of protein and a Gold Standard scoop has 24 g, but the Body Fortress scoop is 45 g of powder against 31 g. Compare cost and calories per 25 g or 30 g of protein, not per scoop, or the bigger scoop will always look better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying Amazon prices for Body Fortress</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Body Fortress is priced for big-box shelves. At the prices we saw, the 3.9 lb chocolate tub cost about $1.70 per 25 g on Amazon against just under $1 at Walmart. If you buy it online, check the seller and compare the store price first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading 60 g on the tub as one scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 60 g figure on the front of Body Fortress tubs is a two-scoop serving of about 90 g of powder and 360 calories. Treating it as one scoop doubles your intake of calories, zinc and vitamin D without you noticing. One scoop gives 30 g of protein, which is plenty for most people at one sitting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Gold Standard tub is sport-certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                ON describes Gold Standard as banned-substance tested and NSF lists it in Certified for Sport, but ON also sells a separately labeled NSF variant. Drug-tested athletes should look for the Certified for Sport logo on the actual tub in their hands and check the product and lot in NSF's database before competing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the small tub to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small tubs are the worst value from both brands. Gold Standard's 2 lb tub cost about $1.77 per 25 g against $1.14 for the 10 lb, and the 1.78 lb Body Fortress tub was about $1.94. Use a small tub to test a flavor, then size up.
              </p>
            </div>
